    "disclosure": "Reuses record.Automatic, record.Exclude, record.Redact; zero model-owned policy means Automatic. Invalid/duplicate/foreign rules and key exclusion/redaction fail before capture or registration.",
    "registration": "audit.Register(..., orm.Auditing(table.Table, policy)); existing per-model write observer factories and instance-owned recorder; no local hook factory needed.",
    "history": "record.Model[M,K] and existing record.FieldChange[V]; nullable and encrypted reader variants use the stored column decoder."
  },
  "contracts": [
    "Capture consumes persisted locked originals/RETURNING snapshots and saved Assigned/Changed flags. It does not run mutators, accessors, equality comparisons or SQL.",
    "Updates, soft deletes and restores retain touched fields plus the key; create and physical delete retain all fields subject to policy. ForceDelete captures once through Deleted. Compare without operation is rejected.",
    "Shared audit/record capture applies exclusions, sensitive-codec/name and encryption redaction before invoking those field binders; JSON redaction occurs before audit-row SQL binding. Untouched fields never invoke capture binders.",
    "Primary identity uses the already declared stored key binder/decoder. Historical reads use the already declared decoder, preserving omitted/absent/NULL/redaction states and custom stored representations.",
    "Automatic auditing stays inside the business transaction. An error from audit or a later observer aborts business and audit rows together. Existing acknowledged set writes skip model observers/audit.",
    "Existing callback.Isolated and safe fault wrapping remain the shared capture/decode boundaries. Metadata bridges validate issued sqlowner.Metadata capabilities before retaining or invoking callbacks; no capability is passed to application code.",
    "No new generic field capture closures or Query/Table methods. Nullable metadata retains its existing decoder function beside its lifecycle policy; audit key adapters only delegate the declared codec functions."
  ],
